# Files inside the assets directory that must NOT be uploaded to Workers.
# Same syntax as .gitignore. Astro copies this file verbatim from public/ to the
# root of dist/, which is where Wrangler looks for it.
#
# It excludes nothing today. The file exists for the opposite reason — to say what
# must never be added to it.
#
#   NEVER add _headers or _redirects.
#
# Cloudflare's own migration guide lists them as candidates, and following that
# advice here would be silent and expensive. On Workers those two files are not
# served as assets at all: they are parsed, and their rules are applied to asset
# responses. Excluding them from the upload does not stop them being public — they
# already are not — it stops them existing. The site would lose its Content
# Security Policy, its cache policy and its playground rewrite, and every page
# would keep answering 200.
#
# scripts/verify-output.sh fails the build if either name appears below.
#
# ---
#
# Also absent, but no longer for the original reason: *.br and *.gz.
#
# The Blazor publish emits a pre-compressed twin of every framework file — 60 of the
# artefact's 196 files, around 3 MiB. Two things had to be true before excluding
# them, and both are now measured rather than argued:
#
#   The loader never asks for a twin. Nothing under _framework/ that fetches assets
#   contains a reference to `.br`.
#
#   The host compresses application/wasm on the way out. Against the deployed site,
#   3,002,102 bytes arrive as 1,217,181 with Content-Encoding: br. The figure is the
#   proof, not the compression: the same payload compresses to 1,197,764 under the
#   local runtime, and two different sizes for one file mean the host compressed it
#   itself rather than serving the twin beside it.
#
# So the twins are dead weight, and the question that kept them is closed.
#
# What is not settled is the exclusion itself. `wrangler deploy --dry-run` does not
# apply this file — its "Read N files" line is identical with these patterns present,
# absent, or excluding the whole playground — so nothing local can demonstrate that
# excluding them uploads fewer files, or that the playground still boots afterwards.
# Adding them on the strength of the documentation alone would be a change whose
# failure mode is a blank playground in production.
#
# The sequence that would settle it, in this order:
#
#   1. add `*.br` and `*.gz` below, and deploy
#   2. curl -sI <url>/playground/_framework/<any>.wasm.br   → expect 404
#   3. scripts/check-served-headers.sh <url>                → expect six ✓
#   4. open /playground/ and watch it start
#
# Step 2 is the one that matters: a 200 there means the twins are still being
# uploaded and nothing was gained. Step 3 is what proves nothing was lost.
